Old `_switchTo`:
```dart
void _switchTo(BuildContext context, String networkId) {
 unawaited(controller.enterNetwork(networkId));
 unawaited(() async {
   try {
     await manager.resume(networkId);
   } on StateError catch (e) {
     if (context.mounted) showFahErrorSnack(context, e.message);
   } on FaNetworkException catch (e) {
     if (context.mounted) showFahErrorSnack(context, e.message);
   } on Object catch (e) {
     if (context.mounted) showFahErrorSnack(context, '$e');
   }
 }());
}
```

New:
```dart
void _switchTo(BuildContext context, String networkId) {
 unawaited(controller.enterNetwork(networkId));
 unawaited(_resumeSwitchedNetwork(context, networkId));
}

Future<void> _resumeSwitchedNetwork(BuildContext context, String networkId) async {
 try {
   await manager.resume(networkId);
 } on Object catch (e) {
   _showResumeError(context, _resumeErrorMessage(e));
 }
```

Check subtlety: old code had per-type catches; the `on Object catch (e)` in old code catches everything not StateError/FaNetworkException. New catches all Object and maps: StateError → e.message; FaNetworkException → e.message; else '$e'. Order of catch clauses: old had StateError first, then FaNetworkException, then Object — since these types are unrelated, mapping is same. `$e` for other types same as toString via interpolation. Note `_resumeErrorMessage` uses `if (e is StateError) return e.message; if (e is FaNetworkException) return e.message; return '$e';` — same.

One subtle difference: old `unawaited(() async {...}())` — closure invoked immediately; new `unawaited(_resumeSwitchedNetwork(...))` — an async method call creates a future immediately, body starts synchronously up to first await. `manager.resume` called in both immediately. `controller.enterNetwork` unawaited first in both. Equivalent.

`context.mounted` — context is the BuildContext captured; in old code, `context.mounted` checked in each catch. New: `_showResumeError` checks context.mounted. Same.

